  <CaseSection id="decision-shipment-choices" title="A few choices on each shipment">
    <CaseFor project="ivfcryo" personas={["lauren"]} stage="request" />
    <CaseProse>
      <p>
        There was a strong push for one-click shipping. Customers would set defaults for
        container size, departure and receiving facilities, and courier instructions, and
        each shipment after that would take one click. That required foresight and setup time
        customers didn’t have. A default that didn’t fit would also have to be undone.
      </p>
      <p>
        I prototyped the workflow with several customers and timed it. Customers supported the
        idea at first. When they used the prototype, one-click shipping took more effort than
        it saved.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <div class="mt-10 rounded-lg border border-line p-6 md:p-8">
      <Heading level={3} visual="h3" class="mb-6">Prototype timings</Heading>
      <ul>
        {oneClickTimings.map((row) => (
          <li class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-6 border-b border-line py-3.5 last:border-b-0">
            <span class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-snug">{row.task}</span>
            <span class="shrink-0 text-sm font-medium text-ink">{row.time}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>

    <CaseProse class="mt-10">
      <p>
        A matching shipment saved about 20 seconds. Customers needed
        <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">about 30 matching shipments to recover the
        setup time</strong>, and most shipped far fewer in a year. Each changed default
        added more time.
      </p>
      <p>
        After a lot of persuading, stakeholders agreed to drop the upfront defaults.
        Customers chose the departure and receiving facilities, container type, and personnel
        for each shipment, and reused instructions saved for each facility. Each shipment took
        four or five clicks. Because customers made each choice themselves, shipments were
        less prone to error.
      </p>
      <p>
        After launch, we reviewed the workflow in Hotjar every week and talked through any
        sticking points. There was a push to get every shipment done in 30 seconds. People often
        start a shipment, get pulled away, and finish it later, so we decided further speed
        work wasn’t worth the cost.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection id="decision-forms" title="Forms that prevent mistakes and let people fix them">
    <CaseFor project="ivfcryo" personas={["lauren","martin"]} stage="delivery" />
    <CaseProse>
      <p>
        Every form lets people fix mistakes without calling support. Addresses are checked
        against map data, and specimen details use structured fields.
      </p>
      <p>
        CRYO Shield, IVFCRYO’s shipping guarantee, pays only on fully documented claims, so
        orders capture declared value and receiving captures a temperature reading and photos.
      </p>
      <p>
        A legally blind customer, the basis for Martin’s persona, had trouble with the older
        shipping form on IVFCRYO’s website. They tested the new forms with a screen reader
        before and after launch. Those sessions turned up
        form errors that weren’t tied to their fields, keyboard problems, and missed
        <span class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">aria-live</span> announcements, as well as the
        labeling issue in <a href="#edges" class="hero-link">Limitations</a>.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <div class="mt-10 rounded-lg border border-line p-6 md:p-8">
